          "text": "NEW: An AI researcher at Anthropic runs a business that hosts \"consensual non-consent\" orgies in the Bay Area. The orgies have been the subject of sexual assault allegations and sparked debate about the AI industry's attitudes toward women, some of whom report feeling pressured to attend the orgies in order to advance their careers.\n\nPaul Crowley, a cybersecurity researcher at Anthropic, is listed as a manager of Red Means No LLC, which says that its parties are \"designed to be spaces to … ignore the 'nos' of others.\" Participants can use a safe word, \"red,\" to end an encounter but give \"default consent\" to \"spanking,\" \"wrestling,\" and \"light pain\" by entering the orgy, according to Red Means No's website. More extreme actions, including \"light electricity\" and \"moderate beatings,\" are \"permitted with negotiation,\" though \"body-shaming and race-shaming … are disallowed entirely.\"\n\nThe orgies have been known to attract a disproportionate number of employees at Anthropic and OpenAI, a dynamic critics say creates professional pressure for women in AI to attend. The criticism spilled into public view last month after an independent AI researcher, John Wentworth, said that he \"absolutely [does] upgrade\" his assessment of AI safety experts \"if I find out they go to [Red Means No],\" adding that attendance was a \"strong signal\" of free thought.\n\n\"Want to get ahead in your AI career? Go to the rape orgy,\" Substacker Ivy Asterix quipped. At least one woman claims to have been sexually assaulted at Red Means No, which the group describes as a \"safe space\" to have \"your resistance ignored.\"🧵\n\nhttps://t.co/bV2tkbKj6I"
